ted. He then repeats the focal avowal, stresses that it is serious, and separately offers her himself, his life, and his soul. Constance again refuses and retreats.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no undertaking. In the adjoining sentence, Lorck separately offers Constance “mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl”; the practical terms are broad and figurative rather than specified. Constance rejects the offer with “Nej, uf nej” and moves away, so there is no uptake or mutual undertaking.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text explicitly identifies the work as Amalie Skram's “Constance Ring.”\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents Lorck as emotionally affected through his prior account of prolonged infatuation, trembling voice, nervous behaviour, and later conduct. It nevertheless cannot conclusively settle the sincerity, durability, or moral character of his professed love, and the exact practical content of his separate offer remains unspecified.\"}"
